<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <ScenTitle>BALTAP - Close down Mukran, 1987</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;P&gt;In a local and surprising offensive the&amp;nbsp;USSR invaded Bornholm.&amp;nbsp;The Soviets declared the Central Baltic as "mare clausum" and "sea of freedom". All NATO ships, submarines and aircraft proceeding east of the line Arkona-Trelleborg (longitude&amp;nbsp;approx. E013°20')&amp;nbsp;will be destroyed.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;For NATO this came as surprise because there was no preparation (i.e.&amp;nbsp;reinforcement of troops) visible. &lt;BR&gt;Third Worldwar did not break out but the tensions are extremly high. There were&amp;nbsp;small air and sea battles until the situation&amp;nbsp;calmed down a little. The territories of West-Germany (FGR) and Denmark were not attacked although some of their military key infrastructure in the baltic area&amp;nbsp;(radar stations, ELINT stations)&amp;nbsp;was destroyed by Soviet commando operations.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;USSR began with concentration of troops in East-Germany (GDR) immediately after the invasion of Bornholm. With the beginning of Soviet troop transports through&amp;nbsp;Poland the Independent Self-governing Labour Union "Solidarity" began with massive demonstrations against their government and destroying railway lines and streets at the border to USSR.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;In order not to lose Poland from the Warsaw Pact, USSR did not enforce the military transport through Poland yet. Hence the reinforcement of troops has to be managed through baltic ferry links and transports through CSSR.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;An air strike on the ferry ports in East-Germany (GDR)&amp;nbsp;is bearing the risk of further escalating the situation.&amp;nbsp;Thus NATO decided to send submarines for covered operations.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Your mission as commander of a group of two submarines&amp;nbsp;will be to&amp;nbsp;mine the harbour entries of Mukran Ferry Port and Sassnitz Harbour.&amp;nbsp;Therefor your Type 206A submarines are fitted with an external minebelt. Embarked SEAL-Teams should destroy the loading bridge in Mukran which is essential for unloading the big, double-decked railway ferries.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;BR&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;FONT color=#0080ff&gt;------Historical Background------&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=#0080ff&gt;&lt;EM&gt;In 1987 the German Democratic Republic and the USSR established a railway ferry service from Mukran (GDR) to Kleipeda (USSR). &lt;BR&gt;The ferries of Type EGF-321&amp;nbsp;were the&amp;nbsp;biggest&amp;nbsp;double-deck railway ferries in the world. The ferries&amp;nbsp;were equipped with&amp;nbsp;railway tracks of Russian&amp;nbsp;broad gauge. In Mukran the gauge was shifted to standard European gauge.&lt;BR&gt;A loading bridge was&amp;nbsp;necessary to load/unload the railway waggons from the upper deck. Mukran was the only East German port with the necessary facilities.&lt;BR&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;EM&gt;Beside their civil duties the ferries were intended for military transports from their layout. Military equipment could be transported either on waggons&amp;nbsp;or as Ro/Ro cargo. There were also&amp;nbsp;secret decks for transport of 300 soldiers on board.&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=#0080ff&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;U&gt;Literature: &lt;BR&gt;&lt;/U&gt;Ostseefähren im Kalten Krieg, W. Klietz, 2012, ISBN 3861536730&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;BR&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;A href="https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/F%C3%A4hrverbindung_Mukran%E2%80%93Klaip%C4%97da"&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;FONT color=#0080ff&gt;https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/F%C3%A4hrverbindung_Mukran%E2%80%93Klaip%C4%97da&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;/A&gt;&lt;BR&gt;&lt;A href="https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Typ_EGF-321"&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;FONT color=#0080ff&gt;https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Typ_EGF-321&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;/A&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
